3. **RRF ablation** (Table 3): RRF ≈ S1-only (gap=0.001). RRF functionally degrades to S1-dominant mode.\n4. **Closed-weight degradation** (Table 4): Without S1, detection AUC collapses to 0.515, MAE +86%, coverage -25pp.\n5. **Dose-response** (Table 5): ARC forgetting at 0.5B = -17.5pp. 1.5B/3B nearly flat. GSM8K 3B non-monotonic (format effect).\n6. **7B bridging** (Table 6): Effects attenuate further. ARC -1.5pp, HumanEval +10.5pp.\n7. **Per-benchmark γ** (Table 7): ARC γ=-1.46, CI [-2.51,-0.41], significant. GSM8K γ=0.19, CI [-0.92,1.33], null. MMLU γ=-0.85, CI [-3.20,1.50], unconverged (R̂=1.08, ESS=85).\n8. **Oracle vs S1** (Table 8): S1 amplifies ARC forgetting 2.4×. GSM8K loses oracle's positive signal (3.77→0.19).\n9. **MAE** (Table 9): Best on ARC (0.035 vs 0.059 raw). But mean MAE (0.052) worse than raw (0.034) and linear regression (0.032).\n10. **Coverage** (Table 10): 44-62.5% at 95% level. Far below nominal.\n11. **Identifiability** (Table 11): At N=16, coverage 45-55% regardless of AUC. N≥30 needed for >80%.\n12. **Coverage decomposition** (Table 13): >70% from insufficient N, ~20-25% local independence violation, <5% measurement error.\n13. **Cross-family** (Tables 14-15): Llama ARC forgetting -14pp. γ correlation r=0.85. MAE +0.3pp, coverage -4.5pp.\n14. **RL observational** (§5.7): Regret improves 12% (2.5pp→2.2pp). CIs 2.25× wider for RL models.\n15. **Appendix**: Noise propagation (insensitive), paraphrase quality (not a confound), expected regret.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe design includes these planned experiments:\n- exp-001: Controlled SFT injection (covered in paper)\n- exp-002: Signal pipeline across all scales (covered: Table 1, Table 2)\n- exp-003: RRF ablation (covered: Table 3)\n- exp-004: 144-condition simulation sweep (covered partially: Table 11-12, but paper says 120 conditions)\n- exp-005: Noise propagation / measurement error model (covered: Appendix A, Table 13)\n- exp-006: IRT calibration + baselines (covered: Tables 7-10)\n- exp-007: 7B scale bridging (covered: Table 6)\n- exp-008: Signal survival analysis (covered in signal effectiveness section)\n- exp-009: RL observational (covered: §5.7)\n- exp-012: 72B observational (covered briefly: §5.7)\n- exp-013: Closed-weight 2-signal (covered: Table 4)\n- exp-014: Multi-family Llama validation (covered: Tables 14-15)\n- exp-015: QC-only paraphrase ablation (covered: Table 16, Appendix B)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper's **methodological soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Address:\n\n1. **IRT model specification**: Is the 4PL+γ model well-motivated? Are the priors reasonable? Is Bayesian estimation appropriate here? Any identifiability concerns beyond what the paper discusses?\n\n2. **Experimental design**: Is the controlled contamination setup valid? Are dosage levels appropriate? Is the scale coverage sufficient? Are baselines comprehensive and fair?\n\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are the evaluation metrics appropriate? Is the coverage gap analysis convincing? Is the Fisher Information argument sound? Are conclusions supported by the evidence?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are random seeds / variance reported?\n\n5. **Weaknesses in methodology**: Any fundamental flaws? Missing controls? Confounds not addressed?\n\n6. **Experiment coverage**: Based on the review context, are there important planned experiments that are missing or underrepresented in the paper?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing experiments or analyses\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- Short justification for score\n","uuid":"507e9955-f8b4-4d17-8526-6fd697334b51","session_id":"9513347c-0cdf-4b9b-9658-b8164fba2e34"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale). Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"ContamCal: Calibrating LLM Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\"\n\nA two-stage framework:\n- **Stage 1**: Compute item-level contamination exposure scores via 3 signals: S1 (embedding similarity, training-agnostic), S2 (Min-K++, token perplexity), S4 (Self-Critique). Fused via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n- **Stage 2**: Fit a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model: P(Y=1) = c_i + (d_i - c_i)·σ(a_i(θ_m + γ_k·e_m - b_i)), where γ_k is per-benchmark contamination coefficient. Bayesian estimation via Stan (NUTS, 4 chains, 1000 warmup, 1000 sampling).\n- **Calibrated score**: Set γ_k=0 in posterior to get decontaminated score with credible intervals.\n- **Fisher Information**: I(γ_k) ∝ N_models × Var(e) × f(AUC). Establishes identifiability thresholds.\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- Controlled SFT injection via LoRA on Qwen2.5 (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B) at 4 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d100) on 4 benchmarks (MMLU 14K items, ARC 1.2K, GSM8K 1.3K, HumanEval 164).\n- 7B scale bridging (Qwen2.5-7B, 5 dosage levels).\n- Cross-family: Llama-3.2-1B at 3 dosage levels.\n- 7 baselines: B0 raw, B1 remove-rescore, B2 2PL+γ, B3 vanilla 4PL, B4 linear regression, B5 Emperor, B6 DCR fuzzy.\n- 6 metrics: MAE, 50/80/95% coverage, CI sharpness, Kendall τ, dose-response monotonicity, PPP.\n\n## Key Results (from paper)\n1. **Signal effectiveness** (Table 1): S1 AUC>0.95 across all 9 scale×benchmark conditions. S2 near chance (0.465-0.513). S4 near chance (0.481-0.519). Failure is method-specific, not scale-dependent.\n2. **Min-K++ directional reversal** (Table 2): Cohen's d = -1.45 to -1.98 at d100. Actively misleading.\n3. **RRF ablation** (Table 3): RRF ≈ S1-only (gap=0.001). RRF functionally degrades to S1-dominant mode.\n4. **Closed-weight degradation** (Table 4): Without S1, detection AUC collapses to 0.515, MAE +86%, coverage -25pp.\n5. **Dose-response** (Table 5): ARC forgetting at 0.5B = -17.5pp. 1.5B/3B nearly flat. GSM8K 3B non-monotonic (format effect).\n6. **7B bridging** (Table 6): Effects attenuate further. ARC -1.5pp, HumanEval +10.5pp.\n7. **Per-benchmark γ** (Table 7): ARC γ=-1.46, CI [-2.51,-0.41], significant. GSM8K γ=0.19, CI [-0.92,1.33], null. MMLU γ=-0.85, CI [-3.20,1.50], unconverged (R̂=1.08, ESS=85).\n8. **Oracle vs S1** (Table 8): S1 amplifies ARC forgetting 2.4×. GSM8K loses oracle's positive signal (3.77→0.19).\n9. **MAE** (Table 9): Best on ARC (0.035 vs 0.059 raw). But mean MAE (0.052) worse than raw (0.034) and linear regression (0.032).\n10. **Coverage** (Table 10): 44-62.5% at 95% level. Far below nominal.\n11. **Identifiability** (Table 11): At N=16, coverage 45-55% regardless of AUC. N≥30 needed for >80%.\n12. **Coverage decomposition** (Table 13): >70% from insufficient N, ~20-25% local independence violation, <5% measurement error.\n13. **Cross-family** (Tables 14-15): Llama ARC forgetting -14pp. γ correlation r=0.85. MAE +0.3pp, coverage -4.5pp.\n14. **RL observational** (§5.7): Regret improves 12% (2.5pp→2.2pp). CIs 2.25× wider for RL models.\n15. **Appendix**: Noise propagation (insensitive), paraphrase quality (not a confound), expected regret.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe design includes these planned experiments:\n- exp-001: Controlled SFT injection (covered in paper)\n- exp-002: Signal pipeline across all scales (covered: Table 1, Table 2)\n- exp-003: RRF ablation (covered: Table 3)\n- exp-004: 144-condition simulation sweep (covered partially: Table 11-12, but paper says 120 conditions)\n- exp-005: Noise propagation / measurement error model (covered: Appendix A, Table 13)\n- exp-006: IRT calibration + baselines (covered: Tables 7-10)\n- exp-007: 7B scale bridging (covered: Table 6)\n- exp-008: Signal survival analysis (covered in signal effectiveness section)\n- exp-009: RL observational (covered: §5.7)\n- exp-012: 72B observational (covered briefly: §5.7)\n- exp-013: Closed-weight 2-signal (covered: Table 4)\n- exp-014: Multi-family Llama validation (covered: Tables 14-15)\n- exp-015: QC-only paraphrase ablation (covered: Table 16, Appendix B)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper's **methodological soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Address:\n\n1. **IRT model specification**: Is the 4PL+γ model well-motivated? Are the priors reasonable? Is Bayesian estimation appropriate here? Any identifiability concerns beyond what the paper discusses?\n\n2. **Experimental design**: Is the controlled contamination setup valid? Are dosage levels appropriate? Is the scale coverage sufficient? Are baselines comprehensive and fair?\n\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are the evaluation metrics appropriate? Is the coverage gap analysis convincing? Is the Fisher Information argument sound? Are conclusions supported by the evidence?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are random seeds / variance reported?\n\n5. **Weaknesses in methodology**: Any fundamental flaws? Missing controls? Confounds not addressed?\n\n6. **Experiment coverage**: Based on the review context, are there important planned experiments that are missing or underrepresented in the paper?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing experiments or analyses\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- Short justification for score\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BdpYc7RWPQ5obBZSrBFUhz","session_id":"9513347c-0cdf-4b9b-9658-b8164fba2e34","uuid":"dc676f9b-9f4f-4577-85a4-c8f6be9685d4","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:22:42.402Z"}
