{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BQN1cdAzGRK2XNp9CA9YL1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my R2 review:\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nContamCal proposes a two-stage framework that bridges contamination detection and benchmark score calibration via a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark contamination coefficients. The paper's most valuable contributions are empirical characterizations -- the Min-K++ directional reversal finding and the benchmark-dependent contamination effect heterogeneity (ARC forgetting vs. GSM8K inflation) -- rather than the calibration machinery itself, which produces worse MAE than raw scores on average and suffers from severe coverage collapse (44-69% at nominal 95%).\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Genuinely novel cross-disciplinary synthesis.** The adaptation of educational measurement's item compromise IRT tradition to LLM evaluation is original in this space. The three structural adaptations (independent examinees to single model, binary to continuous exposure, stable to regime-conditional ability) are clearly articulated and represent non-trivial conceptual work. The connection to DIF literature (Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999) is well-drawn, and the positioning of gamma_k as a uniform DIF parameter is technically apt. No prior work has formalized this bridge between contamination detection outputs and IRT-based score adjustment.\n\n- **Min-K++ directional reversal is a significant, citation-worthy empirical finding.** The discovery that Min-K++ actively misleads at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.45 to -1.98) -- not merely failing but reversing direction -- is novel and practically important. This goes beyond known MIA limitations and has immediate implications for practitioners. The mechanistic link to catastrophic forgetting at small scales is plausible. This single finding would be valuable as a short paper contribution regardless of the IRT calibration results.\n\n- **Benchmark-dependent contamination effects challenge a simplistic narrative.** The finding that ARC exhibits forgetting (gamma = -1.46, CI excludes zero) while GSM8K shows no significant effect (gamma = 0.19) under signal-derived exposure contradicts the assumption that contamination uniformly inflates scores. This is an empirically grounded and useful characterization, even if the calibration built on top of it does not yet work reliably.\n\n- **Fisher Information derivation is technically sound and provides actionable thresholds.** The derivation connecting signal AUC to gamma recoverability (structural threshold AUC >= 0.55, practical AUC >= 0.70) is correct and provides useful guidance. The 120-condition simulation sweep adds empirical validation. The conditional nature (other parameters treated as known) is appropriately caveated.\n\n- **Related work coverage is thorough and fairly positioned.** The paper cites the relevant IRT-for-LLM literature (Lalor et al. 2024, TinyBenchmarks, Growing Pains, ATLAS, HiBayES, PSN-IRT), the contamination detection ecosystem (Min-K++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS, Time Travel), the mitigation literature (Emperor's New Clothes, LiveBench, DCR), and the educational measurement tradition (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Mislevy 1986). The disambiguation between psychometric calibration and probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999) is helpful. The conformal prediction discussion (Angelopoulos & Bates 2023) addresses a natural alternative.\n\n- **Honest framing of negative results.** The paper explicitly does not claim MAE improvement and frames the work as proof-of-concept/diagnostic. This intellectual honesty is commendable, though it creates tension with top-venue standards (see weaknesses).\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **(W1) The core contribution (calibration) does not work, and the paper's title and framing still foreground it.** Mean MAE of 0.052 is worse than raw scores (0.034) and linear regression (0.032). Coverage ranges from 44% to 62.5% at nominal 95%. Only 1 of 4 benchmarks (ARC) shows a significant gamma; MMLU fails to converge entirely. The paper is titled \"Calibrating LLM Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented IRT\" but the calibration demonstrably makes scores less accurate on average. Regardless of how honestly the limitations are discussed, a paper whose headline contribution produces negative results needs to be restructured so that the title and abstract reflect what actually works. The framing pivot from D038 (\"detection + benchmark-dependent characterization\") has not yet been reflected in the paper draft, which still uses the calibration framing throughout.\n\n- **(W2) Novelty ceiling: the IRT model itself is standard explanatory IRT with a single covariate.** The 4PL+gamma model (Eq. 2) is a direct instantiation of explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004) with one external covariate. The per-benchmark gamma_k is achieved simply by fitting separate models per benchmark -- not through a novel hierarchical or structural extension. The measurement error model (Eq. 3) is a standard normal noise model. While the application context is new, the methodological contribution is integration rather than innovation. For a top venue, the method section needs to deliver something that advances IRT methodology itself, not merely applies it to a new domain. The three \"structural adaptations\" are well-articulated but amount to (1) acknowledging a limitation without solving it, (2) using continuous rather than binary covariates (standard), and (3) noting that ability varies across training regimes (also left unmodeled -- handled through the uncertainty channel rather than explicit regime parameters).\n\n- **(W3) RRF fusion adds nothing over S1-only, undermining the multi-signal narrative.** The gap between RRF and S1-only is 0.001 AUC. S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) both have AUC near 0.5 at 0.5B scale. The paper describes a 4-signal pipeline but S3 (ConStat) was never actually computed (D030 confirms abandonment). The method section still describes 4 signals including ConStat, creating a claim-code mismatch. RRF fusion actually degrades results when S2/S4 noise corrupts S1's perfect monotonicity (memory.md documents this: Spearman vs oracle drops from 1.0 with S1-only to 0.7 with RRF). The multi-signal architecture is theoretically motivated but empirically vacuous at the scales tested.\n\n- **(W4) The paper draft is incomplete -- all results tables contain placeholders (\"XX.X\", \"[TBD]\", \"[PLACEHOLDER]\").** The experiments.tex file has zero filled-in numbers. All figures are placeholder boxes. The abstract is a TODO. This makes it impossible to assess the final presentation quality of the results narrative. While I can evaluate the experimental design and framing from the project documentation, the actual paper is not in a reviewable state as a manuscript.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **(W5) 16 tables (planned) with zero figures is a severe presentation imbalance.** The method is inherently visual -- a pipeline diagram, signal AUC heatmap, dose-response curves, and identifiability phase diagram would communicate the story far more effectively than tables alone. The project's own paper_framing.md flags \"zero figures\" as a \"serious deficiency\" identified in Dream D2, but no figures have been produced. Tables 11-19 (as planned) could mostly move to an appendix.\n\n- **(W6) Closed-weight mode collapses to AUC 0.515 (near random).** When S1 (embedding similarity) is unavailable -- which is precisely the scenario for closed-weight commercial models that practitioners most care about -- ContamCal is essentially non-functional. This severely limits practical applicability. The paper should be upfront that ContamCal requires training corpus access (open-weight models only) to be effective.\n\n- **(W7) The ConStat signal (S3) described in the method section was never computed.** Decision D030 confirms S3 was abandoned, yet Section 3.1 of the method still describes it as one of the four signals. This is a factual inconsistency that must be corrected before submission. The method should describe a 3-signal pipeline, or ConStat should be implemented.\n\n- **(W8) Single model family (Qwen2.5) limits generalizability claims.** The cross-family validation with Llama-3.2-1B is at a single scale with only 3 benchmark data points -- insufficient for meaningful correlation analysis. The r=0.88 number (from 3 points) has no statistical power. This is acknowledged but the paper still relies on this correlation to support the \"benchmark property\" interpretation.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Given that calibrated MAE is worse than raw MAE on average, what is the practical use case for ContamCal? Under what conditions would a practitioner choose to use it rather than simply reporting raw scores?\n\n2. The method section describes 4 signals including ConStat (S3), but project records indicate S3 was never computed and was formally abandoned (D030). Which signals were actually used in the results? The paper must be corrected to match reality.\n\n3. For ARC, the negative gamma likely reflects catastrophic forgetting under SFT at 0.5B scale rather than a genuine contamination measurement effect. At 3B, ARC d100 accuracy is 84% (mild forgetting); at 0.5B, it is 29-37% (catastrophic). How do you disentangle SFT capacity displacement from the contamination effect that gamma is supposed to capture?\n\n4. The Fisher Information derivation conditions on all other parameters being known. With N=8-16 models and ~4000+ jointly estimated parameters, what is the actual posterior variance of gamma relative to the Cramer-Rao lower bound?\n\n5. Have you considered a hierarchical gamma specification (gamma_k ~ Normal(mu_gamma, sigma_gamma)) instead of independent per-benchmark fits? This could share information across benchmarks and provide a formal test of heterogeneity.\n\n6. Why not use the 2PL model as primary for open-ended benchmarks (GSM8K) where guessing and slip parameters are not meaningful? The c_j ~ Beta(5,20) prior with mean 0.2 is inappropriate for free-form math problems.\n\n---\n\n## Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n- **Burnell et al. 2023** (multidimensional IRT for LLMs): The unidimensional theta assumption is particularly questionable for MMLU, which spans 57 subjects. This work should be cited when discussing the unidimensional limitation.\n\n- **HELM (Liang et al. 2023)**: As a comprehensive evaluation framework that partially addresses contamination through broad benchmark coverage, it should appear in the related work as an alternative evaluation philosophy.\n\n- **Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023)**: Human preference evaluation that inherently bypasses contamination. Worth mentioning alongside LiveBench as a contamination-free alternative.\n\n- **Data attribution methods (TracIn, TRAK)**: Pruthi et al. 2020 and Park et al. 2023 are mentioned in the paper_framing.md but not yet in the paper. These could serve as alternative/complementary exposure signal sources and should be discussed in Future Work.\n\n- **No comparison to simply using the d000 baseline as the calibrated score** (i.e., the trivially optimal \"oracle\" baseline that the paper's own ground truth provides). While not a fair comparison, reporting it would contextualize how far ContamCal is from the achievable bound.\n\n---\n\n## Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Confidence: High**\n\n---\n\n## Justification\n\nThe paper addresses a timely and important problem -- bridging contamination detection and score calibration -- with a conceptually clean framework grounded in educational measurement theory. The Min-K++ directional reversal finding and benchmark-dependent contamination characterization are genuinely valuable empirical contributions. The Fisher Information analysis provides useful theoretical grounding.\n\nHowever, the core calibration claim is not supported by results: calibrated MAE is worse than raw scores on average, coverage collapses far below nominal levels, and only 1 of 4 benchmarks shows a significant contamination parameter. The methodological contribution (standard explanatory IRT with one covariate) is below the novelty bar for a top venue. The multi-signal pipeline is empirically dominated by S1-only, and one of the four described signals was never implemented. The paper draft itself is incomplete (all results are placeholders, no abstract, no figures).\n\nThe characterization findings alone -- Min-K++ reversal, benchmark-dependent effects, scale-dependent signal reliability, identifiability bounds -- could form a strong contribution if the paper were reframed away from calibration and toward systematic empirical characterization of contamination behavior within an IRT framework. The current framing promises calibration but delivers characterization, creating a claim-evidence mismatch that would be penalized at review. A major revision reframing the narrative around what the results actually show, implementing figures, and resolving the ConStat inconsistency could reach 4/6 (Borderline Accept)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa71a05d7ffb4e990 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa71a05d7ffb4e990' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 82832\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 152760</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9513347c-0cdf-4b9b-9658-b8164fba2e34","uuid":"25186c2e-b477-4226-b702-615a24f7fd75","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:25:41.855Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale). Your focus: **Novelty, Related Work, and Presentation**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"ContamCal: Calibrating LLM Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\"\n\nA two-stage framework bridging contamination detection and score calibration:\n- **Stage 1**: Item-level exposure scores from 3 signals (S1 embedding similarity, S2 Min-K++, S4 Self-Critique) fused via RRF.\n- **Stage 2**: 4PL IRT model augmented with per-benchmark γ_k contamination coefficient. Bayesian estimation via Stan. Calibrated score = posterior prediction with γ_k=0.\n- **Fisher Information**: I(γ_k) ∝ N_models × Var(e) × f(AUC).\n\nThree claimed contributions:\n1. **Signal effectiveness characterization**: Systematic mapping of signal × scale × dosage. S1 (embedding) is the only reliable signal (AUC>0.95). Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.45 to -1.98).\n2. **Contamination-augmented IRT calibration**: Per-benchmark γ_k captures benchmark-dependent effects. ARC forgetting (γ=-1.46, significant), GSM8K null (γ=0.19). Proof-of-concept, not claiming MAE improvement.\n3. **Fisher Information identifiability analysis**: 120-condition simulation sweep. Structural threshold AUC≥0.55, practical AUC≥0.70. Coverage gaps at N=16 dominated by insufficient sample size (>70%).\n\n## Related Work (from paper §2)\n- **Contamination detection**: n-gram matching (Dodge 2021), Min-K++ (Shi 2024), ConStat (DeKoninck 2024), Self-Critique. Paper positions as using detection outputs as inputs to calibration.\n- **IRT for LLM evaluation**: Lalor et al. 2024, TinyBenchmarks (Polo 2024), ATLAS, Growing Pains, HiBayES. None use contamination as a covariate. Paper extends explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004) with γ.\n- **Contamination mitigation**: Xu et al. 2025 (20 strategies, none effective), DCR (Oren 2024, fuzzy matching but no UQ), LiveBench (White 2024, dynamic benchmarks). Paper complements by providing post-hoc calibration with uncertainty.\n- **MIA and data attribution**: Shokri 2017, Carlini 2022, Pruthi 2020, TRAK (Park 2023). Min-K++ reversal connects to MIA literature.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n- Mean MAE (0.052) is WORSE than raw scores (0.034) and linear regression (0.032). The paper explicitly does NOT claim MAE improvement.\n- 95% coverage ranges from 44% to 62.5% — far below nominal.\n- Only 1 of 4 benchmarks (ARC) shows significant γ. MMLU fails to converge entirely.\n- RRF adds nothing over S1-only (gap = 0.001).\n- Closed-weight mode collapses completely (AUC 0.515).\n- RL observational improvement is modest (12% regret reduction).\n- The most valuable finding may be the Min-K++ reversal and the ARC-vs-GSM8K contrast.\n\n## Paper Structure\n13 pages total (10 main + 3 appendix). 16 tables, no figures in the PDF (though figures are described in review context). Sections: Intro, Related Work, Method (3 pages), Experimental Setup (1 page), Results (4 pages with 8 subsections), Discussion, Limitations, Conclusion.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper's **novelty**, **positioning relative to prior work**, and **presentation quality**. Address:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the γ-augmented IRT model? Is the connection to DIF/explanatory IRT genuinely new in the LLM context? Is the signal characterization a sufficient contribution even without strong calibration results?\n\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are important baselines or prior work missing? Is the positioning fair? Specifically check: (a) Are there other IRT+contamination approaches? (b) Is the DIF connection well-established? (c) Are there other calibration frameworks they should compare to?\n\n3. **Claim calibration**: Do the claims match the evidence? The paper explicitly frames as \"proof-of-concept\" and \"diagnostic framework\" rather than claiming superiority. Is this framing honest and sufficient for a top venue?\n\n4. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are the 16 tables too many? Is the narrative clear? Is the \"honest limitation\" framing effective or does it undermine the contribution?\n\n5. **Impact and significance**: Would this paper advance the field? Who would use ContamCal? Is the Min-K++ reversal finding alone citation-worthy?\n\n6. **Missing related work or comparisons**: Any important prior work not cited? Any important baseline comparisons missing?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing related work or comparisons\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- Short justification for score\n","agentId":"aa71a05d7ffb4e990","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my R2 review:\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nContamCal proposes a two-stage framework that bridges contamination detection and benchmark score calibration via a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark contamination coefficients. The paper's most valuable contributions are empirical characterizations -- the Min-K++ directional reversal finding and the benchmark-dependent contamination effect heterogeneity (ARC forgetting vs. GSM8K inflation) -- rather than the calibration machinery itself, which produces worse MAE than raw scores on average and suffers from severe coverage collapse (44-69% at nominal 95%).\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Genuinely novel cross-disciplinary synthesis.** The adaptation of educational measurement's item compromise IRT tradition to LLM evaluation is original in this space. The three structural adaptations (independent examinees to single model, binary to continuous exposure, stable to regime-conditional ability) are clearly articulated and represent non-trivial conceptual work. The connection to DIF literature (Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999) is well-drawn, and the positioning of gamma_k as a uniform DIF parameter is technically apt. No prior work has formalized this bridge between contamination detection outputs and IRT-based score adjustment.\n\n- **Min-K++ directional reversal is a significant, citation-worthy empirical finding.** The discovery that Min-K++ actively misleads at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.45 to -1.98) -- not merely failing but reversing direction -- is novel and practically important. This goes beyond known MIA limitations and has immediate implications for practitioners. The mechanistic link to catastrophic forgetting at small scales is plausible. This single finding would be valuable as a short paper contribution regardless of the IRT calibration results.\n\n- **Benchmark-dependent contamination effects challenge a simplistic narrative.** The finding that ARC exhibits forgetting (gamma = -1.46, CI excludes zero) while GSM8K shows no significant effect (gamma = 0.19) under signal-derived exposure contradicts the assumption that contamination uniformly inflates scores. This is an empirically grounded and useful characterization, even if the calibration built on top of it does not yet work reliably.\n\n- **Fisher Information derivation is technically sound and provides actionable thresholds.** The derivation connecting signal AUC to gamma recoverability (structural threshold AUC >= 0.55, practical AUC >= 0.70) is correct and provides useful guidance. The 120-condition simulation sweep adds empirical validation. The conditional nature (other parameters treated as known) is appropriately caveated.\n\n- **Related work coverage is thorough and fairly positioned.** The paper cites the relevant IRT-for-LLM literature (Lalor et al. 2024, TinyBenchmarks, Growing Pains, ATLAS, HiBayES, PSN-IRT), the contamination detection ecosystem (Min-K++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS, Time Travel), the mitigation literature (Emperor's New Clothes, LiveBench, DCR), and the educational measurement tradition (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Mislevy 1986). The disambiguation between psychometric calibration and probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999) is helpful. The conformal prediction discussion (Angelopoulos & Bates 2023) addresses a natural alternative.\n\n- **Honest framing of negative results.** The paper explicitly does not claim MAE improvement and frames the work as proof-of-concept/diagnostic. This intellectual honesty is commendable, though it creates tension with top-venue standards (see weaknesses).\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **(W1) The core contribution (calibration) does not work, and the paper's title and framing still foreground it.** Mean MAE of 0.052 is worse than raw scores (0.034) and linear regression (0.032). Coverage ranges from 44% to 62.5% at nominal 95%. Only 1 of 4 benchmarks (ARC) shows a significant gamma; MMLU fails to converge entirely. The paper is titled \"Calibrating LLM Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented IRT\" but the calibration demonstrably makes scores less accurate on average. Regardless of how honestly the limitations are discussed, a paper whose headline contribution produces negative results needs to be restructured so that the title and abstract reflect what actually works. The framing pivot from D038 (\"detection + benchmark-dependent characterization\") has not yet been reflected in the paper draft, which still uses the calibration framing throughout.\n\n- **(W2) Novelty ceiling: the IRT model itself is standard explanatory IRT with a single covariate.** The 4PL+gamma model (Eq. 2) is a direct instantiation of explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004) with one external covariate. The per-benchmark gamma_k is achieved simply by fitting separate models per benchmark -- not through a novel hierarchical or structural extension. The measurement error model (Eq. 3) is a standard normal noise model. While the application context is new, the methodological contribution is integration rather than innovation. For a top venue, the method section needs to deliver something that advances IRT methodology itself, not merely applies it to a new domain. The three \"structural adaptations\" are well-articulated but amount to (1) acknowledging a limitation without solving it, (2) using continuous rather than binary covariates (standard), and (3) noting that ability varies across training regimes (also left unmodeled -- handled through the uncertainty channel rather than explicit regime parameters).\n\n- **(W3) RRF fusion adds nothing over S1-only, undermining the multi-signal narrative.** The gap between RRF and S1-only is 0.001 AUC. S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) both have AUC near 0.5 at 0.5B scale. The paper describes a 4-signal pipeline but S3 (ConStat) was never actually computed (D030 confirms abandonment). The method section still describes 4 signals including ConStat, creating a claim-code mismatch. RRF fusion actually degrades results when S2/S4 noise corrupts S1's perfect monotonicity (memory.md documents this: Spearman vs oracle drops from 1.0 with S1-only to 0.7 with RRF). The multi-signal architecture is theoretically motivated but empirically vacuous at the scales tested.\n\n- **(W4) The paper draft is incomplete -- all results tables contain placeholders (\"XX.X\", \"[TBD]\", \"[PLACEHOLDER]\").** The experiments.tex file has zero filled-in numbers. All figures are placeholder boxes. The abstract is a TODO. This makes it impossible to assess the final presentation quality of the results narrative. While I can evaluate the experimental design and framing from the project documentation, the actual paper is not in a reviewable state as a manuscript.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **(W5) 16 tables (planned) with zero figures is a severe presentation imbalance.** The method is inherently visual -- a pipeline diagram, signal AUC heatmap, dose-response curves, and identifiability phase diagram would communicate the story far more effectively than tables alone. The project's own paper_framing.md flags \"zero figures\" as a \"serious deficiency\" identified in Dream D2, but no figures have been produced. Tables 11-19 (as planned) could mostly move to an appendix.\n\n- **(W6) Closed-weight mode collapses to AUC 0.515 (near random).** When S1 (embedding similarity) is unavailable -- which is precisely the scenario for closed-weight commercial models that practitioners most care about -- ContamCal is essentially non-functional. This severely limits practical applicability. The paper should be upfront that ContamCal requires training corpus access (open-weight models only) to be effective.\n\n- **(W7) The ConStat signal (S3) described in the method section was never computed.** Decision D030 confirms S3 was abandoned, yet Section 3.1 of the method still describes it as one of the four signals. This is a factual inconsistency that must be corrected before submission. The method should describe a 3-signal pipeline, or ConStat should be implemented.\n\n- **(W8) Single model family (Qwen2.5) limits generalizability claims.** The cross-family validation with Llama-3.2-1B is at a single scale with only 3 benchmark data points -- insufficient for meaningful correlation analysis. The r=0.88 number (from 3 points) has no statistical power. This is acknowledged but the paper still relies on this correlation to support the \"benchmark property\" interpretation.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Given that calibrated MAE is worse than raw MAE on average, what is the practical use case for ContamCal? Under what conditions would a practitioner choose to use it rather than simply reporting raw scores?\n\n2. The method section describes 4 signals including ConStat (S3), but project records indicate S3 was never computed and was formally abandoned (D030). Which signals were actually used in the results? The paper must be corrected to match reality.\n\n3. For ARC, the negative gamma likely reflects catastrophic forgetting under SFT at 0.5B scale rather than a genuine contamination measurement effect. At 3B, ARC d100 accuracy is 84% (mild forgetting); at 0.5B, it is 29-37% (catastrophic). How do you disentangle SFT capacity displacement from the contamination effect that gamma is supposed to capture?\n\n4. The Fisher Information derivation conditions on all other parameters being known. With N=8-16 models and ~4000+ jointly estimated parameters, what is the actual posterior variance of gamma relative to the Cramer-Rao lower bound?\n\n5. Have you considered a hierarchical gamma specification (gamma_k ~ Normal(mu_gamma, sigma_gamma)) instead of independent per-benchmark fits? This could share information across benchmarks and provide a formal test of heterogeneity.\n\n6. Why not use the 2PL model as primary for open-ended benchmarks (GSM8K) where guessing and slip parameters are not meaningful? The c_j ~ Beta(5,20) prior with mean 0.2 is inappropriate for free-form math problems.\n\n---\n\n## Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n- **Burnell et al. 2023** (multidimensional IRT for LLMs): The unidimensional theta assumption is particularly questionable for MMLU, which spans 57 subjects. This work should be cited when discussing the unidimensional limitation.\n\n- **HELM (Liang et al. 2023)**: As a comprehensive evaluation framework that partially addresses contamination through broad benchmark coverage, it should appear in the related work as an alternative evaluation philosophy.\n\n- **Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023)**: Human preference evaluation that inherently bypasses contamination. Worth mentioning alongside LiveBench as a contamination-free alternative.\n\n- **Data attribution methods (TracIn, TRAK)**: Pruthi et al. 2020 and Park et al. 2023 are mentioned in the paper_framing.md but not yet in the paper. These could serve as alternative/complementary exposure signal sources and should be discussed in Future Work.\n\n- **No comparison to simply using the d000 baseline as the calibrated score** (i.e., the trivially optimal \"oracle\" baseline that the paper's own ground truth provides). While not a fair comparison, reporting it would contextualize how far ContamCal is from the achievable bound.\n\n---\n\n## Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Confidence: High**\n\n---\n\n## Justification\n\nThe paper addresses a timely and important problem -- bridging contamination detection and score calibration -- with a conceptually clean framework grounded in educational measurement theory. The Min-K++ directional reversal finding and benchmark-dependent contamination characterization are genuinely valuable empirical contributions. The Fisher Information analysis provides useful theoretical grounding.\n\nHowever, the core calibration claim is not supported by results: calibrated MAE is worse than raw scores on average, coverage collapses far below nominal levels, and only 1 of 4 benchmarks shows a significant contamination parameter. The methodological contribution (standard explanatory IRT with one covariate) is below the novelty bar for a top venue. The multi-signal pipeline is empirically dominated by S1-only, and one of the four described signals was never implemented. The paper draft itself is incomplete (all results are placeholders, no abstract, no figures).\n\nThe characterization findings alone -- Min-K++ reversal, benchmark-dependent effects, scale-dependent signal reliability, identifiability bounds -- could form a strong contribution if the paper were reframed away from calibration and toward systematic empirical characterization of contamination behavior within an IRT framework. The current framing promises calibration but delivers characterization, creating a claim-evidence mismatch that would be penalized at review. A major revision reframing the narrative around what the results actually show, implementing figures, and resolving the ConStat inconsistency could reach 4/6 (Borderline Accept)."}],"totalDurationMs":152760,"totalTokens":82832,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":547,"cache_read_input_tokens":79170,"output_tokens":3112,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":547},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3112,"cache_read_input_tokens":79170,"cache_creation_input_tokens":547,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":547,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
