{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tj9YG6nX7aQAAeBomkDmg6","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have full context: my previous R2 review (the median tier one from `dream_20260514_R2_novelty`) and R1's review as summarized in the prompt. Let me write my reaction.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Reaction to R1's Review\n\n**Areas of Strong Agreement**\n\nR1's identification of coverage collapse (20-33% even with oracle) as the most devastating weakness fully aligns with my own W2 assessment (95% CI coverage at 69% best-case, 44% on GSM8K). R1 sharpens this by noting that PPP extremes indicate systematic misspecification rather than insufficient N — I agree, and this is a stronger formulation than my original framing. The oracle coverage result is particularly damning because it eliminates signal noise as a root cause, leaving model misspecification as the only explanation.\n\nI also strongly agree with R1's point about S1 dominance rendering the multi-signal pipeline cosmetic. My W3 made the same argument: RRF-4 degrades below S1-only (AUC 0.947 vs 0.975). R1's framing — that S2/S4 noise \"corrupted S1 monotonicity\" through RRF — is a precise mechanistic explanation I should have articulated more clearly.\n\nThe N_models critique (R1's major weakness about 4690 parameters from 8-16 models) matches my W6. R1's phrasing that the paper \"uses N=8 when own theory says N≥30\" is more pointed than my \"N=16 below recommended threshold,\" and correctly so. The internal contradiction between the simulation recommendations and the actual experimental setup is a serious scientific integrity issue.\n\n**Areas of Partial Disagreement**\n\nR1 frames the GSM8K result as \"IRT calibration consistently worse than raw scores (MAE 0.092 vs 0.005 = 18x worse).\" I flagged this same issue (my W4: the only significant gamma is confounded by format learning) but I would characterize the root cause differently. The overcorrection is not a general indictment of IRT calibration — it is specific to a benchmark where gamma confounds format recovery with memorization. R1's blanket framing slightly overstates the problem by not acknowledging that ARC calibration does modestly improve over raw scores.\n\nR1's characterization of the additive gamma model as \"conflating item-level memorization boost with general capability degradation\" is technically correct but I would note this is a known limitation of any single-parameter contamination effect model, not specific to this paper's formulation. The question is whether a more complex specification would actually be identifiable at N=16.\n\n**Points That Strengthen My Assessment**\n\nR1's observation about the simulation sweep being incomplete (120/144 conditions with systematic failures in auc0.65 conditions) is a detail I missed. Systematic failures at the identifiability boundary undermine the very analysis meant to establish that boundary — this strengthens the case for weak reject.\n\nR1's missing experiment suggestions (SBC diagnostic, d100_qc_only ablation, multi-seed pooled IRT at N≥30) are well-targeted and represent work that should have been done before submission.\n\n**Score Adjustment**\n\nR1's review does not change my score. We independently converged on 3/6 with high confidence, which increases my conviction that this is the correct assessment. The paper's strongest contribution remains the Min-K++ reversal finding, which both reviewers identify as genuinely novel but insufficient to carry a paper framed around calibration. Final score: **3/6, unchanged**."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae7df8d0b8c131a7e (use SendMessage with to: 'ae7df8d0b8c131a7e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 31553\ntool_uses: 7\nduration_ms: 42859</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9513347c-0cdf-4b9b-9658-b8164fba2e34","uuid":"867c7b71-23da-40a0-b401-81d206cf97ad","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:26:58.047Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 (Novelty + Related Work focus). You previously gave a score of 3/6. Now read R1's review below and provide a brief reaction (300-400 words): areas of agreement, disagreement, and whether R1's points change your assessment.\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments focus, Score: 3/6)\n\n**Key R1 Strengths identified:**\n- Well-motivated IRT formulation with proper connection to educational measurement\n- Rigorous Bayesian estimation pipeline (CmdStan NUTS, convergence diagnostics)\n- Thorough controlled contamination design (LoRA SFT, 4 dosages, multiple scales)\n- Min-K++ directional reversal is genuinely novel\n- Fisher Information analysis is rigorous\n- Honest reporting of negative results\n- Comprehensive baseline set (7 baselines, 6 metrics)\n\n**Key R1 Weaknesses:**\n- (Major) Coverage collapse is devastating — 20-33% even with oracle exposure. PPP extremes indicate systematic misspecification, not just insufficient N.\n- (Major) IRT calibration consistently worse than raw scores (GSM8K oracle MAE 0.092 vs raw 0.005 = 18x worse).\n- (Major) N_models critically insufficient — 4690 parameters from 8-16 model vectors. Paper's own analysis says N≥30 needed but uses N=8.\n- (Major) S1 dominance renders multi-signal pipeline cosmetic. RRF actually degraded results when S2/S4 noise corrupted S1 monotonicity.\n- (Major) Additive gamma model misspecifies contamination — conflates item-level memorization boost with general capability degradation (ARC forgetting).\n- (Minor) Cross-family validation limited (single family, single scale)\n- (Minor) 3B GSM8K catastrophic forgetting confound from different LoRA hyperparameters\n- (Minor) Paraphrase quality confound partially uncontrolled\n- (Minor) Simulation sweep incomplete (120 not 144 conditions, systematic failures)\n- (Minor) MMLU IRT results unreliable (R̂=1.08, ESS=85)\n\n**R1 Questions:** Practical value if MAE worse than raw? Separate gamma for memorized vs collateral items? Why report at N=8 when own theory says N≥30? Real-world applicability without training data access?\n\n**R1 Missing experiments:** SBC diagnostic, d100_qc_only ablation, multi-seed pooled IRT at N≥30, verbatim contamination baseline, item-level gamma heterogeneity, posterior predictive checks.\n\n**R1 Score: 3/6, High confidence**\n\n## Your Task\nReact to R1's points. Do you agree/disagree? Does anything change your score or assessment? Be specific about which R1 points strengthen or weaken the case.\n","agentId":"ae7df8d0b8c131a7e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have full context: my previous R2 review (the median tier one from `dream_20260514_R2_novelty`) and R1's review as summarized in the prompt. Let me write my reaction.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Reaction to R1's Review\n\n**Areas of Strong Agreement**\n\nR1's identification of coverage collapse (20-33% even with oracle) as the most devastating weakness fully aligns with my own W2 assessment (95% CI coverage at 69% best-case, 44% on GSM8K). R1 sharpens this by noting that PPP extremes indicate systematic misspecification rather than insufficient N — I agree, and this is a stronger formulation than my original framing. The oracle coverage result is particularly damning because it eliminates signal noise as a root cause, leaving model misspecification as the only explanation.\n\nI also strongly agree with R1's point about S1 dominance rendering the multi-signal pipeline cosmetic. My W3 made the same argument: RRF-4 degrades below S1-only (AUC 0.947 vs 0.975). R1's framing — that S2/S4 noise \"corrupted S1 monotonicity\" through RRF — is a precise mechanistic explanation I should have articulated more clearly.\n\nThe N_models critique (R1's major weakness about 4690 parameters from 8-16 models) matches my W6. R1's phrasing that the paper \"uses N=8 when own theory says N≥30\" is more pointed than my \"N=16 below recommended threshold,\" and correctly so. The internal contradiction between the simulation recommendations and the actual experimental setup is a serious scientific integrity issue.\n\n**Areas of Partial Disagreement**\n\nR1 frames the GSM8K result as \"IRT calibration consistently worse than raw scores (MAE 0.092 vs 0.005 = 18x worse).\" I flagged this same issue (my W4: the only significant gamma is confounded by format learning) but I would characterize the root cause differently. The overcorrection is not a general indictment of IRT calibration — it is specific to a benchmark where gamma confounds format recovery with memorization. R1's blanket framing slightly overstates the problem by not acknowledging that ARC calibration does modestly improve over raw scores.\n\nR1's characterization of the additive gamma model as \"conflating item-level memorization boost with general capability degradation\" is technically correct but I would note this is a known limitation of any single-parameter contamination effect model, not specific to this paper's formulation. The question is whether a more complex specification would actually be identifiable at N=16.\n\n**Points That Strengthen My Assessment**\n\nR1's observation about the simulation sweep being incomplete (120/144 conditions with systematic failures in auc0.65 conditions) is a detail I missed. Systematic failures at the identifiability boundary undermine the very analysis meant to establish that boundary — this strengthens the case for weak reject.\n\nR1's missing experiment suggestions (SBC diagnostic, d100_qc_only ablation, multi-seed pooled IRT at N≥30) are well-targeted and represent work that should have been done before submission.\n\n**Score Adjustment**\n\nR1's review does not change my score. We independently converged on 3/6 with high confidence, which increases my conviction that this is the correct assessment. The paper's strongest contribution remains the Min-K++ reversal finding, which both reviewers identify as genuinely novel but insufficient to carry a paper framed around calibration. Final score: **3/6, unchanged**."}],"totalDurationMs":42859,"totalTokens":31553,"totalToolUseCount":7,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11519,"cache_read_input_tokens":19204,"output_tokens":829,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11519},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":829,"cache_read_input_tokens":19204,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11519,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11519,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":2,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":3}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LHaZGn8whi5PtKfNc5cLsq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both cross-reactions. Both reviewers maintain their 3/6 scores. Let me now proceed to Step 3 (convergence) and Step 4 (synthesize the final report).\n\nLet me identify the convergence points and then write the final consolidated report.\n\n**Convergence points (both reviewers agree):**\n1. Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) — both independently and after cross-sharing\n2. Core calibration claim fails: MAE worse than raw scores, coverage collapses\n\n\n3. The Min-K++ reversal stands out as the most publishable insight\n4. The multi-signal pipeline essentially reduces to just the first signal, with RRF contributing nothing\n5. The N=16 sample size is fundamentally limited based on the paper's own methodology\n6. The authors deserve credit for transparently presenting negative findings\n7. The Fisher Information framework is mathematically rigorous\n8. There's potential to reframe this around characterization rather than calibration claims, which could push toward acceptance\n9. Closed-weight mode fails in practice, severely limiting real-world utility\n10. The validation doesn't extend across different model families\n\nThe main tension between us is whether the RRF degradation is a valuable negative result worth documenting or simply another failure point, and R2 has stronger reservations about the novelty claims overall.\n\nNow I'm pulling together the final review synthesis and checking which experiments might be underrepresented—the simulation conditions fall short of the planned scope, and some of the larger model experiments get minimal coverage in the writeup.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3352,"cache_read_input_tokens":77680,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_inp